WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.849 --> 00:00:06.500
“Mathematics will fall first”, to being
solved by AI, that is, said Francois Fleuret

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:07.860
on December 3rd.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:13.309
Now, on December the 13th, DeepMind published
this paper in Nature having found new solutions

00:00:13.309 --> 00:00:17.650
in math and computer science with large language
models or short LLMs.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:23.390
How did large language models that are known
to hallucinate and give so many bogus answers

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:26.119
become such incredible math solvers overnight?

00:00:26.119 --> 00:00:29.570
That’s what we’ll explain in this video.

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:35.320
So, how can it be that LLMs can make scientific
discoveries when they are known to hallucinate

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.680
and produce factually incorrect information?

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:43.110
Well, let’s remember that they are also
quite creative at times.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:48.730
And there is a fundamental difference between
everyday natural language problems and mathematics

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:49.730
or code:

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:56.730
Math and code have the great property of verifiability,
in other words: once we have a correct solution

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:02.230
to a math or coding problem,
we know whether it is correct, as we see that

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:05.750
the code compiles and that the result is what
we expected.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:13.390
So, while LLMs produce piles of garbage,
the pile comes with some nuggets of creativity.

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:18.110
If there only was a way to decide which generations
are garbage and which ones are genius.

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:25.049
Well, there is this new idea in the LLM-space
of investing compute at test time, after the

00:01:25.049 --> 00:01:28.320
generation:
Suppose we have a problem formulated as an

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.960
LLM prompt: Then, we let the model generate
a million answers.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:37.250
Then we let a verifier rate and rank these
million answers.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:42.729
The verifier could be a test we have written,
or even the same model that generated the

00:01:42.729 --> 00:01:45.829
answers, or another capable model.

00:01:45.829 --> 00:01:52.450
And yes, even the same model that generated
the answers will be quite good at rating its

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:57.860
own answers because quality estimation is
much easier than generation.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:02.560
Think of it this way: it is much easier to
say whether this solution is correct or not,

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.400
than come up with it yourself.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:11.740
And since math and code problems are verifiable,
one can use such verification tests to rank

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:13.230
the top solutions.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:17.930
This is a recipe for how to select genius
answers from a wide range of not-so-good model

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:18.930
outputs.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:24.890
The key insight behind the idea of test time
computation is that the model has been training

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:30.540
for months on internet-scale data and has
achieved a certain amount of capability (so

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:36.620
the capacity to perform a task) by transforming
its inputs layer by layer and arriving at

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:37.750
the answer.

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:44.099
But to increase the competency (so the level
of skill on a task), we could give it some

00:02:44.099 --> 00:02:49.230
thinking time during inference, by letting
it produce many answers, like a million, and

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:52.900
rank those and let it make further computation
with those.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:56.120
In other words,
we are replacing the reasoning we were expecting

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.950
from the model parameters,
with brute force computation at inference

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:00.950
time.

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:06.760
Now, how did DeepMind use this idea in their
Nature paper to develop FunSearch, to solve

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:09.660
long standing math and computer science problems?

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:15.110
Well, because of what we said previously about
code verifiability, you maybe are not very

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:20.720
surprised that for solving math and computer
science problems, they expressed them as code.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:25.800
First, a mathematician describes the problem
in code and writes the tests that evaluate

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:29.790
the problem (to tell apart correct from wrong
solutions).

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:34.370
The mathematician also writes an initial program
as a placeholder for the solution.

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:38.250
By doing so, they specify the expected structure
of the solution.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:42.650
So, the whole thing is more like a skeleton
and it’s useful to put prior knowledge we

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:44.430
have about the problem.

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:48.980
Then, an LLM iteratively improves the initial
program.

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:53.540
In principle the procedure could use any LLM
working with code, but the DeepMind authors

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:58.239
used Codey, which is a code LLM built on top
of PaLM 2.

00:03:58.239 --> 00:04:05.530
So now, Codey improves this initial program
by generating many, many (a million) programs.

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:09.470
A lot of them are garbage, but some of them
are great.

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:14.260
The programs are automatically evaluated on
many inputs by the test functions written

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:15.969
by the mathematician.

00:04:15.969 --> 00:04:20.109
For each input, programs get a score and the
final score of the program is an aggregate

00:04:20.109 --> 00:04:21.959
over all inputs.

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:26.900
Code that does not compile, uses more memory
or time than allowed or does not deliver valid

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.289
results,
was discarded.

00:04:29.289 --> 00:04:33.120
But code that gets high scores goes to a programs
database.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:38.150
Then in the next iteration, FunSearch takes
a program from the program database as an

00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:42.260
initial program (which is now better than
the last initialization).

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:46.670
Or rather, they use a couple of these best
programs to few-shot prompt the model, by

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:51.080
showing it a couple of initial programs sorted
by quality.

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:56.479
With these input programs, they repeat the
whole process of generating a million improvements,

00:04:56.479 --> 00:05:01.789
ranking them based on how well they perform
on test inputs and put the best programs into

00:05:01.789 --> 00:05:05.120
the program database,
from which they sample the initial program

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:09.940
in the next iteration again, favoring higher
scoring and shorter programs.

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:14.150
Okay, this was the method, but did they solve
actual problems with this?

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:19.430
Well, the authors wanted to solve the cap
set problem and collaborated with Jordan Ellenberg,

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:23.990
a mathematics professor at the University
of Wisconsin–Madison, known for making an

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:26.940
important breakthrough on the cap set problem.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:32.949
The problem is about finding the largest set
of points (called a cap set) on a high dimensional

00:05:32.949 --> 00:05:35.479
grid, where no three points are on a line.

00:05:35.479 --> 00:05:40.229
What seems to be a weird graph theory problem,
is a model for other problems in extremal

00:05:40.229 --> 00:05:45.050
combinatorics, asking how large or small a
set of objects can be.

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:49.729
Famous mathematician Terence Tao
described this as his favorite open question.

00:05:49.729 --> 00:05:54.259
This problem is extremely hard to brute force
because the number of possibilities to consider

00:05:54.259 --> 00:06:00.690
becomes extremely large super fast: larger
than the number of atoms in the universe,

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:03.240
which are 10^82.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:05.569
Trust me, I’ve counted.

00:06:05.569 --> 00:06:10.520
Now, FunSearch’s solutions were programs
that discovered the largest cap sets for some

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:13.610
settings that were never found before.

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:17.960
The authors say that “This is the largest
increase in the size of cap sets in the past

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:19.400
20 years.”

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:23.180
The authors from DeepMind did not stop at
the cap set problem and went onto the bin

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:25.530
packing problem in computer science.

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:30.509
Here the problem is to determine how to pack
items of different sizes into the smallest

00:06:30.509 --> 00:06:32.240
number of bins.

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:37.199
This has numerous applications in real life,
think of how to pack your suitcases when flying

00:06:37.199 --> 00:06:42.610
overseas, loading containers into ships, or
loading data into your computer memory, or

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:45.110
allocating jobs on a cluster.

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:48.569
Usually, this problem is solved by heuristics
devised by humans.

00:06:48.569 --> 00:06:53.490
But it is hard to find good heuristics for
each specific situation.

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:59.870
FunSearch automatically gave specific programs,
adapted to the specifics of the data and situations,

00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:01.759
outperforming human heuristics.

00:07:01.759 --> 00:07:06.870
Now, FunSearch helping make these breakthroughs
is huge news, if you ask me and I was super

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:13.979
impressed and yes, maybe mathematics is a
domain where AI can be already put to use,

00:07:13.979 --> 00:07:18.630
especially since verifiability is easier than
with other domains.

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:24.740
FunSearch has shown that one can usefully
leverage imperfect and hallucinatory LLMs,

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:30.949
as long as one has the compute to produce
a million of them per iteration, at least.

00:07:30.949 --> 00:07:35.870
Jordan Ellenberg, the mathematician collaborating
with DeepMind on the cap set problem, said

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:41.220
that “The solutions generated by FunSearch
are far conceptually richer than a mere list

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:42.250
of numbers.

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:46.699
When I study them, I learn something.”,
which might mean AI will become a tool for

00:07:46.699 --> 00:07:50.110
mathematicians to aid their discoveries in
the near future.

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:56.750
Here is what the Fields Medalist Hugo Duminil-Copin
said after I asked him about AI in mathematics

00:07:56.750 --> 00:10:40.389
at the last Heidelberg Laureate Forum

00:10:40.389 --> 00:10:42.220
What do you think of FunSearch?

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:46.130
Are you worried of AI taking over mathematicians
jobs?

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:49.110
Should you now switch fields and give up mathematics?

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:54.120
Well, it is on you to decide, but I would
rather say that an interesting time is coming

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:57.700
for mathematics, and it might get very busy
and vibrant there.

00:10:57.700 --> 00:11:03.399
It’s not like AI will solve math by itself,
because these AIs are not independent: remember,

00:11:03.399 --> 00:11:08.430
FunSearch needed the input of the mathematician
and the problem description and tests to be

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:09.800
able to work.

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:15.510
But thanks to AI, you could make use of some
programming skills and solve problems that

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:18.010
you were not able to crack before!

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:19.190
Thanks for watching this far.

00:11:19.190 --> 00:11:23.100
If you want to see more videos like this one,
subscribe to the channel.

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:26.580
See you next time!

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:32.120
Okay, bye!

